The general form of the efficiency curve for a closed circuit transformer is shown in fig. 4. The horizontal distances represent fractions of full secondary load (represented by unity), and the vertical distances efficiency in percentages. The efficiency curve has a maximum value corresponding to that degree of load at which the copper losses in the transformer are equal to the iron losses.

{{smaller block|In the case of modern closed magnetic circuit transformers the copper losses are proportional to the square of the secondary current <math>(\text{I}_2)</math> or to <math>q\text{I}_2^2</math>, where <math>q = \text{R}_1\alpha^2+\text{R}_2</math>; <math>\text{R}_1</math> being the resistance of the primary and <math>\text{R}_2</math> that of the secondary circuit, while <math>\alpha</math> is the ratio of the number of secondary and primary windings of the transformer. Let <math>\text{C}</math> stand for the core loss, and <math>\text{V}_2</math> for the secondary terminal potential difference (R.M.S. value). We can then write as an expression for the efficiency <math>(\eta)</math> of the transformer <math>(\eta = \text{I}_2\text{V}_2/ (\text{C} + q\text{I}_2^2+\text{I}_2\text{V}_2)</math>. It is easy to show that if <math>\text{C}_1</math>, <math>\text{V}_2</math> and <math>q</math> are constants, but <math>\text{I}_2</math> is variable, the above expression for <math>\eta</math> has a maximum value when <math>\text{C} - q\text{I}_2^2 = \text{O}</math>, that is, when the iron core loss <math>\text{C} =</math> the total copper losses <math>q\text{I}_2^2</math>.}}

''Iron and Copper Losses.''—The iron core energy-waste, due to the hysteresis and eddy currents, may be stated in watts, or expressed as a fraction of the full load secondary output. In small transformers of 1 to 3 kilowatts output it may amount to 2 or 3%, and in large transformers of 10 to 50 kilowatts and upwards it should be 1 or less than 1%. Thus the core loss of a 30-kilowatt transformer (one having a secondary output of 30,000 watts) should not exceed 250 watts.
